{"body":"In this pull request, I had created a super simple n-tier solution using a console application as the mock UI for this iteration.  In VS, set the console application as the startup project and run.  It will ask you for the path to the local flat file with the csv data to evaluate, as well as the path to the output file.  The application will tell you when it is finished processing, in which the application can be closed and the output file can be opened for the results.\r\n\r\nI also created a dual entry into the InputFileService to either enter in with a path to a local file (for the console application) or a StreamReader so that it could be extended possibly from another layer such as an API with a file stream.  I didn't go this route for this iteration but I left it open and ready to do so if the business requirements ever asked for it.\r\n\r\nI did contract out the services layer as well for a future DI implementation and removal of the explicit service instantiations inside the application and service layers.  I didn't take the time to do it for this iteration but the thought was there from the beginning to do so eventually.  I also didn't write any unit tests for this example because of it but they could be worked in at a later date.","created_at":"2017-05-09T04:21:47Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/28","merged_at":null,"number":28,"state":"closed","title":"Cash Register Solution for Change Denominations based on flat file CSV"}
{"body":"This app is Kurt Holliday's solution to TrueFit's Cash Register scenario. It is a Python3 app that generates either the smallest quantity or a random quantity of bills or coins. The quantities are randomized when Owed is divisible by three. The currency is set to USD, and it is easy to expand to use other currencies. When Owed is divisible by three, the currency is reordered and the quantity chosen for change is randomized.","created_at":"2017-03-20T18:37:57Z","head_ref":"app","head_repo":"relaxabyte/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/27","merged_at":null,"number":27,"state":"closed","title":"Cash Register App Submission"}
{"body":"See Readme file in CashRegister folder.","created_at":"2017-03-04T21:28:48Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/26","merged_at":null,"number":26,"state":"closed","title":"Completed Assignment"}
{"body":"My cash register application is good to go.","created_at":"2017-02-26T21:45:54Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":"GreenJT/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/25","merged_at":null,"number":25,"state":"closed","title":"CashRegister"}
{"body":"Initial and complete cut of Eric Fink's cash register for Creative Cash\r\nDraw Solutions.  This feature will calculate the minimal amount of\r\nchange to be given to a customer after a transaction has been completed.\r\nIf the amount owed by the customer is divisible by 3, coins are chosen\r\nat random.\r\n\r\nPlease refer to ASSUMPTIONS_NOTES for further notes and any and all\r\nassumptions worked under.","created_at":"2017-02-24T10:18:57Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":null,"html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/24","merged_at":null,"number":24,"state":"closed","title":"Eric Fink - Completed Cash Register"}
{"body":"","created_at":"2017-02-18T04:27:24Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":"pjb4752/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/23","merged_at":null,"number":23,"state":"closed","title":"think this will do it"}
{"body":"## Overview\r\n\r\n* Determines the minimum count of denominations to return to the customer as change and outputs the results in human-readable form\r\n* For fun, the cash register will determine a random number of denominations to return if the amount owed is a multiple of 3. The change will still add up to the correct monetary amount.\r\n* Reads in transactions from `transactions.csv` and writes results to `change.csv`.\r\n\r\n## Technical Notes\r\n\r\nThe `cash_register.rb` program reads in transactions from `transactions.csv` and outputs the resulting change in denominations to the file `change.csv`.\r\n\r\nTo run (this will update `change.csv` based on `transactions.csv`):\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ bundle exec ruby cash_register.rb\r\n```\r\n\r\nTo run specs:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ bundle exec rspec spec\r\n```\r\n\r\nI've also added `rubocop` with just a few cops disabled. Take a look at `.rubyocop.yml` and you can run `rubocop` with just:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ bundle exec rubocop\r\n``` \r\n","created_at":"2017-01-18T19:11:35Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":"coxandrew/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/22","merged_at":null,"number":22,"state":"closed","title":"Calculate correct change to return by denomination"}
{"body":"I decided to use this project as an opportunity to work on my Ruby chops.  I tried to design the object interface with multiple usages in mind so it could operate within the context of a larger application, while still satisfying the needs of specified functionality.  Doing so also allowed for more thorough unit testing.\n\nI also tried to prepare for what I felt were likely change requests from the client.  For instance, the code can be easily tweaked to support more denominations above a dollar (fives, tens, twenties, etc).\n\nLet me know what you think and if you have any questions.\n\nThanks!\n-Andy\n","created_at":"2016-06-14T03:36:19Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":"timecatalyst/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/21","merged_at":null,"number":21,"state":"closed","title":"Ruby implementation by Andy Yochum"}
{"body":"I really got carried away with this but I also really enjoyed it!\n\nSince we met at the React/Redux meetup, I thought it would be fun to build this in React with a Redux store. Of course it was a ton more work than it needed to be, but I don't mind, and it shows off some of my skills. It's a complete, working example. \n\nLet me know what you think and if you have any questions about why I did what I did.\n\nAfter you merge check out the README for more information.\n","created_at":"2016-06-04T20:49:27Z","head_ref":"master","head_repo":"pblackwood/CashRegister","html_url":"https://github.com/TrueFit/CashRegister/pull/20","merged_at":null,"number":20,"state":"closed","title":"Peter's Awesome Cash Register!"}
